The `TextField` component in Adobe Experience Manager (AEM) doesn't natively support displaying columns. However, you can achieve a similar effect using CSS and JavaScript to style the text input field to appear as if it has columns. Here’s a simple example to get you started:

```javascript
const textField = document.querySelector('.your-text-field-class');

// Add a CSS class to style the text field to appear as columns
textField.classList.add('your-column-class');

// Optionally, you can use JavaScript to add some spacing to mimic columns
textField.style.setProperty('--column-spacing', '10px');
```

And in your CSS, you can define the `.your-column-class` to control the appearance:

```css
.your-column-class {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: row;
  justify-content: space-between;
  align-items: center;
  column-gap: var(--column-spacing);
  /* Additional styles as needed */
}
```

Remember to adjust the `--column-spacing` value to fit your design requirements. This approach will help your text field appear as if it has columns, but keep in mind that this is a visual trick and the input value will still be displayed in a single column.

If you need a more advanced solution that involves server-side processing or AEM-specific configurations, please provide more details so I can offer a more tailored solution.

